 # Training Optimization and Initialization
+
+Also called: 3DGS Initialization Policy, 3D Gaussian Splatting Training, GauFRe
+
 ## Overview
-This family covers the initialization strategies and iterative optimization procedures required to reconstruct 3D scenes from 2D images using 3D Gaussian Splatting [3761-3d-gaussian-splatting-training.md]. It solves the problem of converting sparse or dense image collections into a set of differentiable volumetric primitives that can be rendered in real time [3761-3d-gaussian-splatting-training.md]. It differs from implicit neural field methods by optimizing explicit, static or dynamic ellipsoids via differentiable rasterization, achieving faster convergence and rendering speeds [3761-3d-gaussian-splatting-training.md].
+This family defines the strategies for seeding the initial set of $N$ 3D Gaussian primitives and the iterative optimization process that adjusts their parameters to minimize the difference between rendered images and ground truth views. It sets itself apart from NeRF-based methods by optimizing explicit geometric and appearance attributes rather than neural network weights, offering significantly faster training and inference speeds [3761-3d-gaussian-splatting-training.md]. Unlike static initialization methods, it employs adaptive density control to dynamically manage the number and distribution of Gaussians during training [3761-3d-gaussian-splatting-training.md].
+
 ## Shared principles
-All methods in this family optimize a set of $N$ 3D Gaussian primitives [3761-3d-gaussian-splatting-training.md]. Each primitive $i$ is defined by position $\mathbf{p}_i \in \mathbb{R}^3$ [G], rotation $q_i \in \mathbb{R}^4$ (unit quaternion) [G], scale $\mathbf{s}_i \in \mathbb{R}^3$ [G], opacity $\alpha_i \in [0, 1]$ [G], and appearance parameters such as spherical harmonic coefficients $h_i$ [3761-3d-gaussian-splatting-training.md]. The optimization minimizes a loss function $\mathcal{L}$ between rendered images and ground truth views, typically using the Adam optimizer [3761-3d-gaussian-splatting-training.md]. The rendering process involves projecting 3D Gaussians to 2D screen space, sorting by depth, and alpha blending [3761-3d-gaussian-splatting-training.md].
+All methods in this family optimize a set of $N$ 3D Gaussian primitives [3761-3d-gaussian-splatting-training.md]. Each primitive $i$ is defined by:
+*   Position $\mathbf{p}_i \in \mathbb{R}^3$
+*   Rotation $q_i \in \mathbb{R}^4$ (unit quaternion)
+*   Scale $\mathbf{s}_i \in \mathbb{R}^3$
+*   Opacity $\alpha_i \in [0, 1]$
+*   Appearance parameters (e.g., spherical harmonic coefficients $h_i$) [3761-3d-gaussian-splatting-training.md]
+
+The optimization minimizes a loss function $\mathcal{L}$ between rendered images and ground truth views, typically using the Adam optimizer [3761-3d-gaussian-splatting-training.md]. The rendering process involves projecting 3D Gaussians to 2D screen space, sorting by depth, and alpha blending [3761-3d-gaussian-splatting-training.md].
