 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [3DGS Initialization Policy](1398-3dgs-initialization-policy.md)
-A deterministic strategy for placing $N$ Gaussians within a bounding volume. It sets apart from heuristic methods by using a fixed power-law scaling for initial opacity to ensure consistent integrated opacity regardless of $N$ [1398-3dgs-initialization-policy.md]. Choose this for reproducible baselines in synthetic scenes.
+The 3DGS Initialization Policy defines the strategy for seeding the initial set of $N$ 3D Gaussian primitives before optimization begins. It addresses the problem of sparse or incorrect initial geometry, which can lead to poor convergence or artifacts in the final radiance field. Unlike dynamic densification strategies that spawn primitives during training based on view-space gradients, this policy acts once before optimization using the initial scene geometry or random distribution. It sets apart from standard random initialization by potentially incorporating specific opacity scaling laws derived from order-independent transparency (OTS) formulations [1398-3dgs-initialization-policy.md].
 
 ### [3D Gaussian Splatting Training](3761-3d-gaussian-splatting-training.md)
-The core iterative optimization loop for static scenes. It initializes Gaussians from Structure-from-Motion (SfM) points and uses adaptive density control (splitting/cloning/pruning) to refine geometry [3761-3d-gaussian-splatting-training.md]. Choose this for high-fidelity reconstruction of static scenes from image collections.
+3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) Training is the iterative optimization process that adjusts the parameters of a set of 3D Gaussian primitives to minimize the difference between rendered images and ground truth views [3761-3d-gaussian-splatting-training.md]. It solves the problem of reconstructing photorealistic 3D scenes from 2D images without relying on neural network weights, instead optimizing explicit geometric and appearance attributes [3761-3d-gaussian-splatting-training.md]. It sets itself apart from NeRF-based methods by offering significantly faster training and inference speeds while achieving comparable image quality [3761-3d-gaussian-splatting-training.md]. Unlike static initialization methods, it employs adaptive density control to dynamically manage the number and distribution of Gaussians [3761-3d-gaussian-splatting-training.md].
 
 ### [GauFRe](7994-gaufre.md)
-A method for dynamic scenes that decouples static and dynamic components. It initializes points into static and dynamic subsets and optimizes them independently, using compact MLPs for deformation [7994-gaufre.md]. Choose this when the scene contains significant static background and moving foreground elements.
+GauFRe is a method for real-time dynamic novel view synthesis that represents dynamic scenes using deformable 3D Gaussian primitives [7994-gaufre.md]. It solves the problem of reconstructing non-rigid motion from monocular video by employing a forward-warping deformation field, which explicitly models the temporal evolution of scene geometry [7994-gaufre.md]. Unlike backward-warping approaches common in NeRFs that require querying a canonical space, GauFRe predicts the deformed primitive system at a specific time directly, making it efficient for explicit primitive-based rendering [7994-gaufre.md]. It distinguishes itself by decoupling static and dynamic regions into separate Gaussian sets and applying exponential/normalization constraints to scale and rotation parameters for stable optimization [7994-gaufre.md].
 
 ### Training Optimization
-Joint optimization of network weights, feature grids, and latent codes using the total training loss [S1]. Learnable parameters include $\Theta_s$ (SDF model), $\Theta_d$ (DDF model), $M$ (SDF and DDF feature grids, $M = (M_s, M_d)$), $M_s = \{M^s_i \mid i = xy, yz, zx\}$, $M_d = \{M^d_i \mid i = pxpy, \dots, rzrx\}$, and $Z$ (set of latent codes for $J$ training shapes, $Z = \{z_i \mid i = 1 \dots J\}$) [S1]. Loss weights are $w_s, w_d, w_\sigma, w_{tv}, w_{ts}, w_l$ [S1].
+#### definition
+- Joint optimization of network weights, feature grids, and latent codes using the total training loss [S1].
+#### parameter
+- $\Theta_s$: Learnable parameters of SDF model [S1].
+- $\Theta_d$: Learnable parameters of DDF model [S1].
+- $M$: SDF and DDF feature grids, $M = (M_s, M_d)$ [S1].
+- $M_s$: SDF feature grids, $M_s = \{M^s_i \mid i = xy, yz, zx\}$ [S1].
+- $M_d$: DDF feature grids, $M_d = \{M^d_i \mid i = pxpy, \dots, rzrx\}$ [S1].
+- $Z$: Set of latent codes for $J$ training shapes, $Z = \{z_i \mid i = 1\dots J\}$ [S1].
+- $w_s, w_d, w_\sigma, w_{tv}, w_{ts}, w_l$: Weights for respective losses [S1].
 
 ## Background
-3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [3761-3d-gaussian-splatting-training.md]. The initialization policy using power-law opacity scaling was detailed in [1398-3dgs-initialization-policy.md]. GauFRe was proposed to address optimization instability in dynamic scenes by separating static and dynamic components [7994-gaufre.md].
+The foundational 3D Gaussian Splatting method was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023, establishing the explicit primitive-based rendering pipeline [3761-3d-gaussian-splatting-training.md]. The initialization policy utilizing opacity scaling laws was detailed in subsequent analyses of the 3DGS training dynamics [1398-3dgs-initialization-policy.md]. GauFRe extended this framework to dynamic scenes in 2024, introducing forward-warping deformation fields [7994-gaufre.md]. The "Training Optimization" variant referencing SDF/DFD grids originates from the FIRe method for inverse rendering [S1].
